/* book: lead: A buck converter can only step down and a boost can only step up. A buck-boost converter gets both from one inductor: the switch charges it from the source, then the switch opens and the inductor pushes that energy into the output. The output comes out inverted, and the same circuit works below or above the input voltage. The Cuk converter reaches the same gain with a capacitor carrying the energy */
/* book Figure 3.5.1: The worked example: 12 V in, D = 0.6, −18 V out. With the switch on, the source charges L; with it off, L drives its current up through the load and the diode, so the output node is pulled below the return rail. The Cuk does the same job with C1 at 30 V. */
