/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An isolated converter puts a transformer between the switch and the load.
   - With the switch ON, the input voltage appears across the primary, primary current ramps, and the secondary diode is reverse biased.
   - Vo is the output DC voltage; Vs is the input DC voltage; D is the duty ratio, the fraction of the switching period the switch is on; n = N1/N2 is the primary-to-secondary turns ratio.
   - The switch in a flyback holds the input voltage plus the reflected output voltage: stress = Vs + n·Vo, plus a spike from leakage inductance.
   - A forward converter passes energy while the switch is ON.
   - Vo = Vs·D·(N2/N1)
   - Vo is the output DC voltage; Vs is the input DC voltage; D is the duty ratio; N2/N1 is the secondary-to-primary turns ratio.
   - The core must be reset every cycle.
   - The choice is mostly power level and cost.
   - Above a few hundred watts, push-pull, half-bridge and full-bridge converters drive the transformer both ways, so the core uses both halves of its B-H loop and needs no reset winding.
*/
